# Stacking: position_stack

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`position_stack()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/position_stack.html), which covers
`position_fill()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`position_stack()` stacks bars, areas and other objects that share an `x` on top of one another;
`position_fill()` stacks them too, then stretches every stack to the same height, so its parts
show proportions. A stack runs in the reverse order of the `group` aesthetic, so its colours line
up with the default legend, and positive values stack upward from zero while negative ones stack
downward. `vjust` places an object that has a position rather than a size, such as a label,
within its part of the stack, and `reverse` turns the order over. Giggly stacks bars: it is the
default `position: :stack` of `Giggly.Plot.geom_bar/3` and `Giggly.Plot.geom_col/3`. It has none
of the rest yet: no `position_fill()`, no options, and no stacking of other layers.

## Stacking and filling bars

```r
# Stacking and filling ------------------------------------------------------

# Stacking is the default behaviour for most area plots.
# Fill makes it easier to compare proportions
ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl), fill = factor(vs))) +
  geom_bar()
ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl), fill = factor(vs))) +
  geom_bar(position = "fill")
```

Not in Giggly yet: an expression in a mapping, such as `factor(cyl)`, mapping `fill` and the
legend that reads it back, and `position_fill()`.

## Stacking and filling histograms

```r
ggplot(diamonds, aes(price, fill = cut)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 500)
ggplot(diamonds, aes(price, fill = cut)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 500, position = "fill")
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, `geom_histogram()` with the
binning it counts by, `stat_bin()`, and `position_fill()`.

## Stacked areas

```r
# Stacking is also useful for time series
set.seed(1)
series <- data.frame(
  time = c(rep(1, 4),rep(2, 4), rep(3, 4), rep(4, 4)),
  type = rep(c('a', 'b', 'c', 'd'), 4),
  value = rpois(16, 10)
)
ggplot(series, aes(time, value)) +
  geom_area(aes(fill = type))
```

R's `rpois()` draws the values at random, the same ones every time after `set.seed(1)`.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`, and mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back.

## Setting the stacking order

```r
# Stacking order ------------------------------------------------------------
# The stacking order is carefully designed so that the plot matches
# the legend.

# You control the stacking order by setting the levels of the underlying
# factor. See the forcats package for convenient helpers.
series$type2 <- factor(series$type, levels = c('c', 'b', 'd', 'a'))
ggplot(series, aes(time, value)) +
  geom_area(aes(fill = type2))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`, mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, and choosing
the order of a column's values.

## Ordering the legend

```r
# You can change the order of the levels in the legend using the scale
ggplot(series, aes(time, value)) +
  geom_area(aes(fill = type)) +
  scale_fill_discrete(breaks = c('a', 'b', 'c', 'd'))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`, mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, and
`scale_fill_discrete()`, here to set the order of the legend's keys.

## Reversing the stack

```r
# If you've flipped the plot, use reverse = TRUE so the levels
# continue to match
ggplot(series, aes(time, value)) +
  geom_area(aes(fill = type2), position = position_stack(reverse = TRUE)) +
  coord_flip() +
  theme(legend.position = "top")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`, mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, choosing the
order of a column's values, `position_stack()`'s `reverse` option, `coord_flip()`, and changing
the theme, here to put the legend above the plot.

## Stacking lines and points

```r
# Non-area plots ------------------------------------------------------------

# When stacking across multiple layers it's a good idea to always set
# the `group` aesthetic in the ggplot() call. This ensures that all layers
# are stacked in the same way.
ggplot(series, aes(time, value, group = type)) +
  geom_line(aes(colour = type), position = "stack") +
  geom_point(aes(colour = type), position = "stack")
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `colour` and the legend that reads it back, and `geom_line()`'s and
`geom_point()`'s `position` option, here `position = "stack"`.

## A line on each area

```r
ggplot(series, aes(time, value, group = type)) +
  geom_area(aes(fill = type)) +
  geom_line(aes(group = type), position = "stack")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`, mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, and
`geom_line()`'s `position` option, here `position = "stack"`.

## Stacking labels

```r
# You can also stack labels, but the default position is suboptimal.
ggplot(series, aes(time, value, group = type)) +
  geom_area(aes(fill = type)) +
  geom_text(aes(label = type), position = "stack")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`, mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, and
`geom_text()` and mapping `label`, stacked with `position = "stack"`.

## Centring labels

```r
# You can override this with the vjust parameter. A vjust of 0.5
# will center the labels inside the corresponding area
ggplot(series, aes(time, value, group = type)) +
  geom_area(aes(fill = type)) +
  geom_text(aes(label = type), position = position_stack(vjust = 0.5))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`, mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, `geom_text()`
and mapping `label`, and `position_stack()`'s `vjust` option.

## Negative values

```r
# Negative values -----------------------------------------------------------

df <- data.frame(
  x = rep(c("a", "b"), 2:3),
  y = c(1, 2, 1, 3, -1),
  grp = c("x", "y", "x", "y", "y")
)

ggplot(data = df, aes(x, y, group = grp)) +
  geom_col(aes(fill = grp), position = position_stack(reverse = TRUE)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0)
```

R's `rep()` repeats `"a"` twice and `"b"` three times. The one negative value stacks downward
from zero, apart from the positive ones.

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, `position_stack()`'s
`reverse` option, and `geom_hline()`.

## Labels in the middle of each part

```r
ggplot(data = df, aes(x, y, group = grp)) +
  geom_col(aes(fill = grp)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0) +
  geom_text(aes(label = grp), position = position_stack(vjust = 0.5))
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `fill` and the legend that reads it back, `geom_hline()`,
`geom_text()` and mapping `label`, and `position_stack()`'s `vjust` option.
